@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Influence and Intellectual Relations -- Lenin, Soviet Diamat,
        Labriola, and Western Marxism (Georgi Plekhanov)
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-07-16
PAPERS_FOUND: 14 total (High: 6, Medium: 6, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain traces Plekhanov's downstream influence and lateral
relations rather than his own philosophical sources (covered
separately). Four threads structure the literature. First, Lenin's
philosophical debt to and possible departure from Plekhanov: Lenin's
1908 "Materialism and Empirio-Criticism" adopted Plekhanov's
reflection theory of knowledge and his polemical stance toward
"Machism." Whether Lenin later broke with this framework is itself
contested: Bonilla (2020) reads Lenin's 1915 commentaries on Hegel's
"Science of Logic" as developing a more dialectical, reciprocal
conception of reflection than the 1908 text, while Oittinen (2018)
argues the opposite -- that there is no textual evidence Lenin
abandoned his 1907 positions after encountering Hegel, and that
Lenin's governing idea of dialectics as "concrete analysis of a
concrete situation" derives not from Plekhanov at all but from the
Narodnik writer Alexander Herzen. Second, Plekhanov's role, mediated
through Lenin, in the canonization of Soviet "dialectical materialism"
(diamat) as party orthodoxy -- a process extended by later Soviet
theorists (Mikhail Lifshitz) who continued to elaborate reflection
theory within the Soviet philosophical canon. Third, comparative
relations with other Second International theorists, especially
Antonio Labriola, whose anti-positivist "philosophy of praxis" reached
Russian Social Democrats abroad and, via Gramsci, engaged the debates
in which Plekhanov was a central figure (Savino 2018). Fourth, the
reception and critique of Plekhanov's legacy within Western Marxism
and Marxology: council-communist critics (Pannekoek, Korsch) attacked
the philosophical foundations of Leninism inherited from Plekhanov's
materialism; this critique was transmitted into the Frankfurt School
(Marcuse's "Soviet Marxism") and into the broader post-war
Marxological literature represented by Kolakowski's "Main Currents of
Marxism," Colletti's critique of the "Marxism of the Second
International" (explicitly Kautsky's and Plekhanov's), and Acton's
systematic philosophical critique of "Marxism-Leninism." Georg Lukacs
occupies a distinctive position: his 1924 study of Lenin and his
broader "History and Class Consciousness" offered a Hegelian-Marxist,
praxis-oriented reading of Leninism that recent scholarship (Le Blanc
2013) argues was a coherent Leninist-Trotskyist political position
rather than the "ultra-left" or "proto-Stalinist" deviation it is
often labeled.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the material for assessing how far the "Plekhanov
orthodoxy" was retained, revised, or repudiated as it passed through
Lenin into Soviet diamat and was subsequently judged by rival Marxist
and non-Marxist traditions -- directly addressing the review's
question of Plekhanov's downstream influence and intellectual
relations as distinct from his own sources.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
English-language scholarship specifically comparing Plekhanov and
Labriola side-by-side (rather than via Gramsci as an intermediary) is
thin; likewise, no dedicated recent study of Plekhanov's direct
reception among Kautsky and Mehring (covered instead, for the
political-organizational relationship, in the political-trajectory
domain) was found addressing the properly philosophical dimension of
that relationship. Bykova (2018), Mareeva (2016), Lukacs (1924/1971),
Kolakowski (1978), and Acton (1955) could not be enriched with an
abstract from any available source and are marked INCOMPLETE; their
annotations rely on cross-verified bibliographic description and
context from other entries rather than a first-party abstract, and
they should be treated cautiously (or independently verified) in
synthesis.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize around the "transmission and mutation" of Plekhanov's
reflection-theoretic materialism: (1) Plekhanov to Lenin, including the
live scholarly disagreement over whether a substantive break occurred
after 1914-15 (Bonilla vs. Oittinen), (2) Lenin/Plekhanov to Soviet
diamat orthodoxy, (3) lateral relation to Labriola's praxis-philosophy
via Gramsci, and (4) the Western Marxist/Marxological verdict (Lukacs's
Hegelian-praxis alternative; Pannekoek/Korsch/Frankfurt School
critique; Kolakowski/Colletti/Acton as the post-war Marxological
synthesis of that critique).

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Continuity thesis (no substantive break in Lenin's dialectics, and/or
  Plekhanov not the true source of Lenin's method): Oittinen 2018,
  Pateman 2019, Antonov 2021 -- emphasizes continuity and defends
  Lenin's epistemology against charges of dogmatism or crude
  reflectionism.
- Rupture thesis (Lenin's Hegelian turn after 1914 as a real
  philosophical departure): Bonilla 2020 -- locates a substantive shift
  toward a more reciprocal, dialectical reflection theory in Lenin's
  later Hegel notebooks.
- Western Marxist/council-communist critique: Savin 2020, Le Blanc
  2013, Lukacs 1971[1924], Szabados & Sautkin 2021 -- treats Lukacs's
  Hegelian-praxis Leninism as a considered alternative to the
  Plekhanov-derived materialist orthodoxy, and Pannekoek's critique as
  the origin of the Western "anti-Leninist" reading transmitted via
  the Frankfurt School.
- Post-war Marxological synthesis: Kolakowski 1978, Colletti 1971,
  Acton 1955 -- historical/philosophical reconstructions that place
  Plekhanov's and Kautsky's codification of "dialectical materialism"
  at the origin of the Marxism-Leninism later criticized as scientistic
  or incoherent.
- Soviet diamat afterlife: Mareeva 2016 -- traces the reflection theory
  bequeathed by Plekhanov/Lenin into later Soviet aesthetic philosophy
  (Lifshitz).
====================================================================
}

@article{savin2020origins,
  author = {Savin, Alexey E.},
  title = {Origins of the Interpretation and Criticism of Philosophical Foundations of Leninism in Western Marxism},
  journal = {Vestnik Tomskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta},
  year = {2020},
  number = {458},
  pages = {79--84},
  doi = {10.17223/15617793/458/9},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs the origins of "left communist" (Raetekommunismus) criticism of Leninism in 1920s-30s Germany, Holland, and Denmark, centered on Anton Pannekoek's "Lenin as Philosopher," a sustained critique of Lenin's "Materialism and Empirio-Criticism." The critique holds that Lenin's (and, behind him, Plekhanov's) natural-scientific materialism is a bourgeois-Enlightenment inheritance rather than genuine historical materialism, and traces how this reading was transmitted to the Frankfurt School chiefly via Herbert Marcuse's "Soviet Marxism" (1958), becoming the standard Western-left framing of Soviet dialectical materialism.

  RELEVANCE: Documents the specific genealogy by which the philosophical materialism codified by Plekhanov and adopted by Lenin became, for council communists and later the Frankfurt School, the paradigmatic target of Western Marxist anti-Leninism, directly answering the domain's question about Plekhanov's reception and critique within Western Marxism.

  POSITION: Intellectual-historical reconstruction of the council-communist/Frankfurt School critique of Leninist (and by extension Plekhanovite) philosophical materialism.
  },
  keywords = {Western-Marxism-reception, Lenin, Pannekoek, Frankfurt-School, High},
  abstract = {The aim of the article is to discover the nature of the widespread criticism of Leninism in Western countries in the "left communism" or "communism of the Soviets" (Raetekommunismus), which arose in Germany, Holland, and Denmark in the 1920s and 1930s. To understand the general lines of the criticism of the philosophy of Leninism the author analyzes the ideas presented in the work Lenin as Philosopher by Anton Pannekoek, one of the greatest thinkers and politicians of the "communism of the Soviets". In its philosophical part, the work is devoted to the criticism of Lenin's main philosophical work Materialism and Empirio-Criticism. The author also takes into account the articles devoted to this criticism by Karl Korsch and Paul Mattik, other founders of "communism of the Soviets". The significance of these works is determined by the fact that they constitute the philosophical foundation of contemporary Western "Marxist anti-Leninism". The author reveals the political presuppositions and the political background of the polemic about the philosophical foundations of Leninism. The background is a polemic about the significance of the Russian revolution and the principles of building the Bolshevik party for the rest of the world and especially for Western countries and their Communist parties. The philosophical polemic with Leninism grows out of a doubt about the universal significance of the experience of the Russian revolution. In particular, Pannekoek and Korsch put forward the thesis of the bourgeois-democratic, not socialist character of the Russian revolution. From this thesis, they conclude that the theoretical basis of the Russian revolution is also of a bourgeois character, i.e. the Russian revolution is based on the ideas of the Enlightenment. The philosophical foundation of the Enlightenment is natural-scientific materialism, not historical materialism, i.e. not Marxism. The article demonstrates the genesis of the concept of Leninism as (1) an anti-democratic tendency in the contemporary liberation movement, (2) an instrument for legitimizing the repressive practices of the bureaucracy in the workers' parties and in the "catching-up" states of organized capitalism, (3) a naturalistic mishmash of natural-scientific and historical materialism, ultimately suppressing and emasculating the historicity of Marxist thought. The author reveals how this concept was transmitted to modern Western left-wing thought through the Frankfurt school, and especially through Marcuse's work Soviet Marxism (1958), which for many years became the most popular theoretical source for the Marxist criticism of Soviet dialectical materialism in the Western left.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{pateman2019dogmatism,
  author = {Pateman, Joe},
  title = {Lenin without Dogmatism},
  journal = {Studies in East European Thought},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {71},
  number = {2},
  pages = {99--117},
  doi = {10.1007/s11212-019-09325-6},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Challenges the standard charge that Lenin's epistemology in "Materialism and Empirio-Criticism" grounds the dogmatism and intolerance of twentieth-century Marxism-Leninism. Through textual analysis, Pateman argues Lenin defends a fallibilist conception of scientific knowledge -- affirming uncertainty in natural, philosophical, and social science -- that is inconsistent with the "vulgarizer" caricature routinely applied to him.

  RELEVANCE: Directly engages the reception-and-critique question at the center of this domain by contesting the widely repeated charge (originating partly in Western Marxist and liberal critiques of the Plekhanov-Lenin materialist lineage) that the reflection-theoretic epistemology inherited from Plekhanov necessarily produces dogmatic Marxism-Leninism.

  POSITION: Revisionist defense of Lenin's (and implicitly Plekhanov's) epistemological legacy against the Western critical consensus represented by Pannekoek, Acton, and Kolakowski.
  },
  keywords = {Lenin, epistemology, reflection-theory, reception-debate, Medium},
  abstract = {A longstanding criticism of Lenin is that his epistemological contributions to the theory of scientific socialism prompted the decline of Marxism in dogmatism and despotism in the twentieth century. According to this narrative, Lenin claimed to possess the objective truth, and he therefore refused to tolerate alternative perspectives. This article subjects these claims to a textual analysis, and it argues that they are erroneous. Lenin defends a fallibilist account of science that affirms the uncertainty of knowledge in the natural, philosophical and social sciences. This fallibilism pervades the text that supposedly showcases his intolerance, Materialism and Empirio-Criticism. Lenin's scientific socialism opposes rigid conceptions of socialism, and it encourages an atmosphere of democratic debate. Whilst Lenin's writings have their flaws, they are not the founding documents of Marxist dogmatism that they are often portrayed as being. Lenin is not the narrow-minded vulgariser of Marxism that he is routinely taken to be.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{savino2018italian,
  author = {Savino, Giovanni},
  title = {Italian Marxism and Russian Socialism: Labriola, Gramsci and Philosophical and Cultural Questions of Revolutionary Movement (1890--1937)},
  journal = {Philosophy. Journal of the Higher School of Economics},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {II},
  number = {2},
  pages = {99--117},
  doi = {10.17323/2587-8719-2018-ii-2-99-117},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Analyzes the points of contact between Italian Marxist theorists (Antonio Labriola, Antonio Gramsci) and Russian Socialist/Communist figures (Plekhanov, Lenin, Trotsky). Argues that Labriola was a significant theoretical authority for Russian Social Democrats abroad, that his rejection of positivism and reformism within the Italian Socialist Party influenced debates in other European socialist parties, and that Gramsci's concept of praxis and rejection of positivism derive from Labriola while also engaging Russian Marxism.

  RELEVANCE: Supplies the principal evidence for comparing Plekhanov's philosophical orientation with Labriola's anti-positivist "philosophy of praxis," showing that the connection between the two traditions ran substantially through Labriola's broader influence on Russian emigre Social Democracy rather than through direct textual engagement between Plekhanov and Labriola themselves.

  POSITION: Comparative intellectual history situating Plekhanov within the wider Second International debate over positivism and historical materialism that also produced Labriola's and Gramsci's philosophy of praxis.
  },
  keywords = {Labriola, Second-International, Gramsci, comparative, High},
  abstract = {The aim of this paper is to discuss and analyze the relationship between Italian Marxist thinkers Antonio Labriola and Antonio Gramsci on the one side and prominent representatives of the Russian Socialist and Communist movement on the other: Georgiy Plekhanov, Vladimir Lenin, and Leon Trotsky. In the history of the Socialist (and Anarchic) movement in Italy and in Russia from the end of 19th to the beginning of 20th century, there are many reasons for a more careful study of the points of contact and coincidence between two countries: first of all, because of the existence of the Italian Socialist Party influential leaders of Russian origin, such as Anna Kulisheva (known in Italy as Anna Kuliscioff) and Angelica Balabanova (Angelica Balabanoff). Italian philosopher Antonio Labriola was a significant moral and theoretical authority in the intellectual formation of the Russian socialistic democrats abroad. The influence of Labriola on the European labor movement and on the debate on historical materialism was enormous, and his rejection of the positivism and reformism in the Italian Socialist Party also had an influence on the discussions in the European socialist parties. Antonio Gramsci, the founder of the Italian Communist Party, assimilated the concept of praxis theory, as well as the rejection of positivism and the reformism of Italian socialism from Labriola; Gramsci's idea of the creative power of the working class in the revolutionary process derives from Labriola's arguments about classes in historical materialism. Gramsci's interest in Labriola is associated not only with the role of the philosopher in the creation of the theory of praxis, or his teaching on historical materialism, but also with his influence on Russian Marxism.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{bonilla2020reflexion,
  author = {Bonilla Bonilla, Manuel Alejandro},
  title = {La otra teoría de la reflexión de Lenin},
  journal = {Verinotio -- Revista on-line de Filosofia e Ciências Humanas},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {26},
  number = {1},
  pages = {263--277},
  doi = {10.36638/1981-061x.2020.v26.481},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines the theory of reflection Lenin developed in his 1915 commentaries on Hegel's "Science of Logic," distinct from the reflection theory of "Materialism and Empirio-Criticism" (1908). In the later notebooks, Lenin presents reflection as an adaptive, reciprocal relation between the movement of knowledge and the movement of reality, in which reflection is a (mental) moment of the human process of objectification that transforms reality and constitutes the human being -- a more dialectical formulation than the earlier copy-theory epistemology.

  RELEVANCE: Provides direct evidence for the domain's question about what Lenin retained versus revised from Plekhanov: the 1908 reflection theory closely tracks Plekhanov's materialist epistemology, while the 1915 Hegel notebooks mark, on Bonilla's reading, a substantive philosophical departure toward a more praxis-oriented, dialectical conception. Note that this "rupture" reading is directly contested by Oittinen (2018, this domain), who denies any such break occurred -- the two papers should be read together as opposing positions in a live scholarly disagreement.

  POSITION: Textual-philosophical argument for an internal evolution/rupture in Lenin's epistemology, complicating any simple continuity narrative from Plekhanov through Lenin to Soviet diamat.
  },
  keywords = {Lenin, reflection-theory, Hegel, epistemological-break, High},
  abstract = {Este artigo discute a teoria do reflexo desenvolvida por Lenin em seu confronto com a filosofia hegeliana nos comentarios que escreveu a The Science of Logic em 1915. Nesses comentarios, Lenin apresenta a reflexao no contexto de uma relacao reflexiva adaptativa entre o movimento da o conhecimento e o movimento da realidade; a dinâmica do conhecimento segue o movimento da realidade e a influencia. Para Lenin desses comentarios, a reflexao e um momento (mental) do processo de objetificacao humana que transforma a realidade e origina o ser humano.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{antonov2021matter,
  author = {Antonov, Alexey V.},
  title = {Lenin and Empiriocritics about Matter. Is the Discussion Over?},
  journal = {Vestnik of Saint Petersburg University. Philosophy and Conflict Studies},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {37},
  number = {1},
  pages = {16--29},
  doi = {10.21638/spbu17.2021.102},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Revisits the early-twentieth-century philosophical discussion between Lenin and the "empiriocritics" (the Machist current Plekhanov and Lenin both opposed) over the definition and mode of existence of matter. Argues that Lenin considered the question only in its ontological and gnoseological aspects, and that his own principle of the identity of dialectics, logic, and theory of knowledge requires supplementing the discussion with a logical aspect, which should lead to a correction of Lenin's definition of matter.

  RELEVANCE: Engages directly with the philosophical content of the anti-empiriocritical polemic that Plekhanov initiated and Lenin systematized in "Materialism and Empirio-Criticism," testing the durability of the reflection-theoretic definition of matter that became foundational to Soviet diamat.

  POSITION: Internal philosophical critique that treats the Plekhanov/Lenin definition of matter as still-contested rather than settled orthodoxy.
  },
  keywords = {Lenin, empiriocriticism, matter, diamat, Medium},
  abstract = {The problem of matter in philosophy has become relevant again. Nowadays, it does not resemble the crisis of the early XX century when a so-called "dissolution" of the matter in mathematical formulae was discussed. However, the emergence of term "dark matter" in physics, which is not clear how it differs from the well-known "light matter", is obscure and raises the question of the "matter doubling". This makes one remember that the current definition of matter in philosophy was developed as a result of a discussion concerning the ways of its existence between Vladimir Lenin and empiriocritics. Thus, all modern attempts to define matter cannot exist without a new view on the old discussion presented in the book "Materialism and Empirio-Criticism" by Lenin. The author of the article pays attention to the fact that during the discussion of "the main question of philosophy" a century ago, concerning the attitude of matter towards consciousness, it was considered by Lenin only in two aspects, specifically, ontological and gnoseological. At the same time, the principle of the identity of dialectics, logic and theory of knowledge proposed by him demands consideration of the arguments adduced in the discussion as well as the logical aspect. According to the author, the consecutive consideration of the main question of philosophy by means of logic, should result in a correction of Lenin's definition of matter.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{bykova2018crisis,
  author = {Bykova, Marina F.},
  title = {Lenin and the Crisis of Russian Marxism},
  journal = {Studies in East European Thought},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {70},
  number = {4},
  pages = {235--247},
  doi = {10.1007/s11212-018-9313-5},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Addresses Lenin's role in, and response to, a perceived crisis within Russian Marxist theory in the early twentieth century, situating his philosophical interventions (including the polemic against empirio-criticism) within the wider factional and doctrinal disputes of Russian Social Democracy that Plekhanov had shaped as the movement's original theoretical authority. Content could not be verified against an abstract; this description is based on the title alone and should be checked against the full text before use.

  RELEVANCE: Bears on the domain's question of what in Lenin's philosophical outlook was inherited from, versus developed against, Plekhanov's founding formulations, but cannot be confirmed without the full text.

  POSITION: Contribution to the historiography of Russian Marxist philosophy's early-twentieth-century crisis and Lenin's place within it.
  },
  keywords = {Lenin, Russian-Marxism, INCOMPLETE, Low, no-abstract}
}

@book{kolakowski1978maincurrents,
  author = {Kolakowski, Leszek},
  title = {Main Currents of Marxism: Its Rise, Growth, and Dissolution},
  year = {1978},
  publisher = {Clarendon Press},
  address = {Oxford},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: A three-volume history of Marxism (The Founders, The Golden Age, The Breakdown) reconstructing the development of Marxist thought from its philosophical origins through the Second International to Soviet Marxism-Leninism and Western Marxism; multiple independent contemporary reviews (Parkinson 1979 in Philosophy; Jay 1980 in American Historical Review; Liebich 1986 in Canadian Journal of Philosophy) confirm its three-volume structure and 1978 Clarendon Press publication, and its treatment of Plekhanov's codification of "dialectical materialism" is a standard point of reference in subsequent Plekhanov scholarship. No abstract could be independently verified for the primary text itself.

  RELEVANCE: Functions as the reference Marxological synthesis against which more specialized studies of Plekhanov's influence and reception are typically positioned; its tripartite narrative (rise, golden age, breakdown) is repeatedly invoked in the secondary literature as framing Plekhanov's role as originator of the orthodox Marxist canon that Lenin inherited and Soviet diamat subsequently ossified.

  POSITION: Post-war Marxological history that treats the trajectory from Plekhanov's materialism through Leninism to Stalinist diamat as central to Marxism's eventual "breakdown."
  },
  keywords = {Marxology, Western-reception, historiography, diamat, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{colletti1971marxism,
  author = {Colletti, Lucio},
  title = {The Marxism of the Second International},
  journal = {Telos},
  year = {1971},
  volume = {1971},
  number = {8},
  pages = {84--91},
  doi = {10.3817/0671008084},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Defines "the Marxism of the Second International" narrowly as the Marxism of its two major theoreticians, Kautsky and Plekhanov, while noting that much of this current can already be found in Engels's late works ("Ludwig Feuerbach," "The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State," and the 1895 introduction to "Class Struggles in France"), situating Plekhanov's codification of dialectical materialism as a continuation rather than an independent departure from late Engelsian materialism.

  RELEVANCE: Names Plekhanov, alongside Kautsky, as one of the two defining theoreticians of "Second International Marxism," providing a precise textual anchor (rather than a vague periodizing label) for assessing how Western Marxist critical theory identified and evaluated Plekhanov's specific contribution to the philosophical tradition it judged responsible for the Marxism-Leninism to follow.

  POSITION: Western Marxist critique that traces the philosophical lineage from late Engels through Kautsky and Plekhanov as a continuous current later subjected to Hegelian-Marxist criticism.
  },
  keywords = {Second-International, Western-Marxism-critique, Kautsky, High},
  abstract = {The very notion of "Marxism of the Second International" is a loose one which requires precise definition. It will be used here in a limited sense, i.e., as the Marxism of the two major Marxist theoreticians of the period: Kautsky and Plekhanov. This "practical" definition should not be taken to mean that the Marxism of the Second International is a self-contained phenomenon clearly defined with respect to the First International and to what followed. Actually, much of the Marxism of the Second International can be found in Engels' last works: Ludwig Feuerbach; The Origins of the Family, Private Property and the State; and the 1895 "Introduction" to the new edition of Class-Struggles in France.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{acton1955illusion,
  author = {Acton, H. B.},
  title = {The Illusion of the Epoch: Marxism-Leninism as a Philosophical Creed},
  year = {1955},
  publisher = {Cohen and West},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: A systematic philosophical critique of Marxism-Leninism as a coherent creed encompassing dialectical and historical materialism; independently confirmed by three contemporary reviews (British Journal for the Philosophy of Science 1955; Philosophy 1956; Cambridge Journal 1956) as a 1955 Cohen & West, London publication of viii+278 pages. No abstract could be independently verified for the primary text itself, and an automated retrieval initially attached an unrelated book's review (on a different "Illusion..."-titled work) to this entry; that erroneous content has been removed.

  RELEVANCE: A landmark instance of the non-Marxist Anglo-American philosophical critique of the Plekhanov-Lenin materialist tradition as it hardened into Soviet orthodoxy, useful for contrasting the internal (Western Marxist) and external (liberal/analytic) receptions of that tradition; further verification of its specific arguments against the reader is recommended before use in synthesis.

  POSITION: Cold War-era liberal-analytic critique treating Marxism-Leninism's philosophical foundations (substantially inherited from Plekhanov via Lenin) as internally inconsistent.
  },
  keywords = {Marxism-Leninism-critique, diamat, liberal-critique, INCOMPLETE, High, no-abstract}
}

@article{leblanc2013spider,
  author = {Le Blanc, Paul},
  title = {Spider and Fly: The Leninist Philosophy of Georg Lukács},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2013},
  volume = {21},
  number = {2},
  pages = {47--75},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206x-12341298},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Lukacs's key works of 1923-9 ("History and Class Consciousness," "Lenin: A Study in the Unity of His Thought," "Tailism and the Dialectic," and "The Blum Theses") form a consistent, coherent variant of Leninism, aligned with the political orientation Lenin and Trotsky advanced at the Third and Fourth Congresses of the Communist International, during Lukacs's own leadership (with Jeno Landler) of a faction opposing Bela Kun's ultra-left sectarianism in the Hungarian Communist Party. Contests influential readings that label these works ultra-leftist or proto-Stalinist, and notes that after his political defeat Lukacs renounced practical revolutionary politics and adapted to Stalinism, redirecting his efforts to literary criticism and philosophy.

  RELEVANCE: Reframes Lukacs's reception of Lenin as a considered, practically-engaged Leninist political position rather than an abstract philosophical alternative to Plekhanov's materialism, showing that Western Marxism's most influential Hegelian reading of Lenin originated in concrete factional politics within the Comintern rather than purely in philosophical disagreement with Plekhanovite orthodoxy.

  POSITION: Revisionist account situating Lukacs's 1920s Leninism as politically orthodox (in the Lenin-Trotsky sense) rather than an ultra-left or proto-Stalinist deviation.
  },
  keywords = {Lukacs, Western-Marxism, Lenin, Comintern, Medium},
  abstract = {From 1919 to 1929, the great Hungarian Marxist philosopher Georg Lukács was one of the leaders of the Hungarian Communist Party, immersed not simply in theorising but also in significant practical-political work. Along with labour leader Jenö Landler, he led a faction opposing an ultra-left sectarian orientation represented by Béla Kun (at that time also associated with Comintern chairman Zinoviev, later aligning himself with Stalin). If seen in connection with this factional struggle, key works of Lukács in this period -- History and Class Consciousness (1923), Lenin: A Study in the Unity of His Thought (1924), Tailism and the Dialectic (1926) and 'The Blum Theses' (1929) -- can be seen as forming a consistent, coherent, sophisticated variant of Leninism. Influential readings of these works interpret them as being ultra-leftist or proto-Stalinist (or, in the case of 'The Blum Theses', an anticipation of the Popular Front perspectives adopted by the Communist International in 1935). Such readings distort the reality. Lukács's orientation and outlook of 1923-9 are, rather, more consistent with the orientation advanced by Lenin and Trotsky in the Third and Fourth Congresses of the Communist International. After his decisive political defeat, Lukács concluded that it was necessary to renounce his distinctive political orientation, and completely abandon the terrain of practical revolutionary politics, if he hoped to remain inside the Communist movement. This he did, adapting to Stalinism and shifting his efforts to literary criticism and philosophy. But the theorisations connected to his revolutionary politics of the 1920s continue to have relevance for revolutionary activists of the twenty-first century.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{szabadossautkin2021lukacs,
  author = {Szabados, Bettina and Sautkin, Aleksandr},
  title = {Intellectual Evolution of Georg Lukács in the Context of Developing Lenin's Idea of Revolution},
  journal = {Filosofija. Sociologija},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {32},
  number = {1},
  doi = {10.6001/fil-soc.v32i1.4379},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Traces Lukacs's path from aesthetic essayist to Marxist theorist, showing how, after the 1918 turn to Marxism and the failure of the 1919 Hungarian Soviet Republic, Lukacs drew on Lenin's ideas of revolution to formulate his positions in "History and Class Consciousness" (1923) and "Lenin: A Study on the Unity of his Thought" (1924).

  RELEVANCE: Documents the specific intellectual mechanism by which Lenin's political-philosophical ideas were absorbed into Western Marxism through Lukacs, offering a contrasting case to the Plekhanov-Lenin materialist lineage: Lukacs's Lenin is read through Hegelian categories of totality and praxis rather than through reflection-theoretic materialism.

  POSITION: Intellectual-biographical account of Lukacs's Leninism as a distinctively Hegelian-Marxist current within Western Marxism's reception of Lenin.
  },
  keywords = {Lukacs, Lenin, Western-Marxism, Medium},
  abstract = {The Hungarian philosopher, Georg Lukács, recognized the crisis-consciousness of his generation at the turn of the 20th century and began to search for solutions in the art. However, his theory about art proved to be illusory, being unable to reshape society. After many philosophical attempts to realize his theory in praxis, in 1918 Lukács turned to Marxism and later became one of the most influential Marxist thinkers of his time. In 1919, when the Hungarian Soviet Republic fell, Lukács, like many other intellectuals, had to flee abroad. In emigration, he started to summarize the experiences of the fallen revolution and referred to Lenin's ideas in his early Marxist works, in 'History and Class Consciousness' (1923) and 'Lenin: A Study on the Unity of his Thought' (1924). This paper aims to follow Lukács' path, how he became a Marxist from an Essayist, and what solutions he found for the social crises of his era.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

